\\ CODEBUG.SEQ          A CODE debugger for F-PC        by Tom Zimmer
       ( Modifications for floating point by Bob Smith )
\\                                                      F-PC native port

  This file contains a simple debugger for F-PC to allow debugging
CODE routines. Don't expect TOO MUCH from this debugger, it is still
in quite primitive form. It is useful though.

  The debugger can be invoked directly by:

        TRACE <forth_name> <enter>

  Each subsequent enter key press causes the debugger to step one
instruction through <forth_name>. Limited operations are supported while
in the debugger, read the screen while debugging and press F1 for some help.

  To set a break point that will invoke the debugger when a particular
word is executed, use:

        BREAKAT <forth_name> <enter>

  The first time this word is executed, the code debugger will be started.
If your break point is not ever executed, you should execute UNBREAK
before leaving F-PC to remove the break point from memory.

 ***************************************************************************

  Native port: CODE words are abstract-machine code (SRC/AMASM.SEQ), not
8086 code, and there is no INT 1 single step or INT 3 break point. This
debugger now steps abstract-machine ops with a simulator written in Forth,
with the same semantics as the VM's AM interpreter (vm/am.c), and shows
each op with the disassembler of DISASSEM.SEQ.

  - TRACE <name> runs a CODE word, or a word made by a ;CODE defining
    word, one op at a time from the interpreter.
  - BREAKAT <name> (or XX <name>) changes the word's code token so that
    the next time it runs (from anywhere) it is stepped instead; the break
    point is then removed. UNBREAK removes it, .BREAK shows it.
  - The data stack is copied into a private stack for the simulation, and
    copied back when the word ends (NEXT,). The return stack is private
    too, and starts empty: what the word leaves there is pushed on the
    real return stack when it ends, but a CODE word that pops more than it
    pushes can't be followed (you are told so). The IP the word leaves is
    where execution goes on, as in the VM.
  - W holds the word's CFA, UP the user area, IP the caller's IP. R0-R7
    start at 0. Stepping stops at the NEXT, that starts a >H ... H> group
    (CODEHIGH.SEQ); the high level words and the rest of the word then run
    at full speed.
  - The display is plain lines (registers, the stack, the next op), so it
    works in --batch mode too. Keys:
        SPACE or Enter  do one op              ESC or D  run to the end
        G   go till an address (in hex)        R   set a register
        A   abandon (ABORT)                    F1 or ?   help
  - The 8087 registers and the floating point display are gone (floating
    point is a VM built-in, not code to step).

{

\ **************************************************************************
\ Load the disassembler, we need it in the debugger
\ **************************************************************************

DEFINED AM-STEP NIP
#IF     cr .( Already loaded!) \S
#THEN

NEEDS DISASSEM.SEQ

ONLY FORTH ALSO DISASSEMBLER ALSO DEFINITIONS   \ the debugger's insides

\ **************************************************************************
\ variables to hold all needed registers
\ **************************************************************************

CREATE AMREGS   13 CELLS ALLOT          \ R0-R7 SP RP IP W UP
VARIABLE AMPC                           \ the op about to be done
VARIABLE AMDONE                         \ the word did NEXT,

: REG@  ( r -- n )      CELLS AMREGS + @ ;
: REG!  ( n r -- )      DUP 12 =        \ UP is read only
                        IF 2DROP ELSE CELLS AMREGS + ! THEN ;

: REG=  ( r | <name> -- )               \ defining word to make register
        CREATE , DOES> @ REG! ;         \ assignment words, ( n -- )

FORTH DEFINITIONS               \ these are typed at the R command

 0 REG= =R0   1 REG= =R1   2 REG= =R2   3 REG= =R3
 4 REG= =R4   5 REG= =R5   6 REG= =R6   7 REG= =R7
 8 REG= =SP   9 REG= =RP  10 REG= =IP  11 REG= =W

DISASSEMBLER DEFINITIONS

\ The private stacks: 256 data cells, 64 return cells, each with a guard
\ area of zeros above it (popping an empty stack reads those).
256 CONSTANT #SIMDS
 64 CONSTANT #SIMRS
CREATE SIMDS    #SIMDS 16 + CELLS ALLOT   SIMDS #SIMDS CELLS + CONSTANT SIMDS-TOP
CREATE SIMRS    #SIMRS 16 + CELLS ALLOT   SIMRS #SIMRS CELLS + CONSTANT SIMRS-TOP

: GUARDS-OFF    ( -- )  SIMDS-TOP 16 CELLS ERASE  SIMRS-TOP 16 CELLS ERASE ;

: >SIM  ( x[n-1] .. x0 n -- )   \ move n items of the real stack to SIMDS
        DUP CELLS SIMDS-TOP SWAP - DUP 8 REG!
        SWAP 0 ?DO TUCK ! CELL+ LOOP DROP ;

\ **************************************************************************
\ The abstract machine, one op at a time (as am_run in vm/am.c)
\ **************************************************************************

VARIABLE IA   VARIABLE IB   VARIABLE IC   VARIABLE IMM

: A@    ( -- n )   IA @ REG@ ;
: B@    ( -- n )   IB @ REG@ ;
: CC@   ( -- n )   IC @ REG@ ;
: A!    ( n -- )   IA @ REG! ;
: JUMP  ( f -- )   IF IMM @ AMPC ! THEN ;

: SAR   ( n c -- n' )  31 UMIN 0 ?DO 2/ LOOP ;
: SHIFT ( n c xt -- n' )  OVER 32 U< IF EXECUTE ELSE 2DROP DROP 0 THEN ;

: ?DROOM  ( a -- a )    DUP SIMDS U< ABORT" Private data stack full" ;
: ?RROOM  ( a -- a )    DUP SIMRS U< ABORT" Private return stack full" ;
: AMPOP   ( r -- n )
        DUP REG@ DUP @ SWAP CELL+ ROT REG! ;

: AM-DIVMOD ( -- )
        CC@ 0= ABORT" Division by zero (AM DIVMOD,)"
        B@ S>D CC@ FM/MOD               ( rem quot )
        SWAP IB @ REG!  A! ;

: AM-OP ( op -- )               \ do one op, operands in IA IB IC IMM
        CASE
          0 OF  AMDONE ON                                 ENDOF
          1 OF  IMM @ A!                                  ENDOF
          2 OF  B@ A!                                     ENDOF
          3 OF  B@ CC@ + A!                               ENDOF
          4 OF  B@ CC@ - A!                               ENDOF
          5 OF  B@ CC@ AND A!                             ENDOF
          6 OF  B@ CC@ OR A!                              ENDOF
          7 OF  B@ CC@ XOR A!                             ENDOF
          8 OF  B@ CC@ * A!                               ENDOF
          9 OF  B@ CC@ ['] LSHIFT SHIFT A!                ENDOF
         10 OF  B@ CC@ ['] RSHIFT SHIFT A!                ENDOF
         11 OF  B@ CC@ SAR A!                             ENDOF
         12 OF  B@ IMM @ + A!                             ENDOF
         13 OF  B@ INVERT A!                              ENDOF
         14 OF  B@ NEGATE A!                              ENDOF
         15 OF  B@ IMM @ + @ A!                           ENDOF
         16 OF  B@ IMM @ + C@ A!                          ENDOF
         17 OF  B@ IMM @ + W@ A!                          ENDOF
         18 OF  A@ B@ IMM @ + !                           ENDOF
         19 OF  A@ B@ IMM @ + C!                          ENDOF
         20 OF  A@ B@ IMM @ + W!                          ENDOF
         21 OF  8 REG@ CELL- ?DROOM DUP 8 REG!  A@ SWAP ! ENDOF
         22 OF  8 AMPOP A!                                ENDOF
         23 OF  9 REG@ CELL- ?RROOM DUP 9 REG!  A@ SWAP ! ENDOF
         24 OF  9 AMPOP A!                                ENDOF
         25 OF  TRUE JUMP                                 ENDOF
         26 OF  A@ 0= JUMP                                ENDOF
         27 OF  A@ 0<> JUMP                               ENDOF
         28 OF  A@ B@ = JUMP                              ENDOF
         29 OF  A@ B@ <> JUMP                             ENDOF
         30 OF  A@ B@ < JUMP                              ENDOF
         31 OF  A@ B@ < 0= JUMP                           ENDOF
         32 OF  A@ B@ U< JUMP                             ENDOF
         33 OF  A@ B@ U< 0= JUMP                          ENDOF
         34 OF  AM-DIVMOD                                 ENDOF
         35 OF  B@ CC@ UM* NIP A!                         ENDOF
         36 OF  A@ B@ CC@ CMOVE                           ENDOF
        ENDCASE ;

: AM-STEP ( -- )                \ do the op at AMPC
        AMPC @ @ DUP VALID? 0=
        IF      DROP TRUE ABORT" Bad AM instruction"
        THEN
        DUP RA IA !  DUP RB IB !  DUP RC IC !
        AMPC @ CELL+ @ IMM !
        DUP OPLEN AMPC +!               \ step past it, then do it
        OPCODE AM-OP ;

\ **************************************************************************
\ show the current registers, the stack, and the next op
\ **************************************************************************

: .REGS  ( -- )
        CR 4 0 DO ." R" I 1 .R ." =" I REG@ H.8 SPACE LOOP
        CR 8 4 DO ." R" I 1 .R ." =" I REG@ H.8 SPACE LOOP
        CR ." SP=" 8 REG@ H.8 ."  RP=" 9 REG@ H.8 ."  IP=" 10 REG@ H.8
        ."  W=" 11 REG@ H.8 ."  UP=" 12 REG@ H.8 ;

: DEBUG_DEPTH   ( -- n1 )       \ items on the private data stack
        SIMDS-TOP 8 REG@ - CELL / ;

: BP-DEPTH-DEBUG ( -- n )       \ items on the private return stack
        SIMRS-TOP 9 REG@ - CELL / ;

: DEBUG.S       ( -- )
        CR ." Stack: " DEBUG_DEPTH DUP 0<
        IF      DROP ." underflow"
        ELSE    DUP ." [" 1 .R ." ] " 8 MIN DUP 0
                ?DO     8 REG@ OVER 1- I - CELLS + @ .
                LOOP    DROP
        THEN
        BP-DEPTH-DEBUG ?DUP IF ."   Return stack: " . THEN ;

: .CINST         ( -- )          \ display current instruction
        AMPC @ PC !  ON> SHOW-ADDR .INSN  OFF> SHOW-ADDR ;

: SHOW_DEBUG    ( -- )
        .REGS DEBUG.S .CINST ;

\ **************************************************************************
\ Additional MINI help for the debugger.
\ **************************************************************************

: SHOW_HELP     ( -- )
        CR ."   Debugger commands:"
        CR ."   SPACE or Enter = Do a single instruction"
        CR ."   ESC or D       = Done, run to the end of the word"
        CR ."   G              = Go till an address (hex) is reached"
        CR ."   R              = Set a Register, e.g. $23 =R0"
        CR ."   A              = Abandon: restore the stack and ABORT"
        CR ."   Registers are =R0 .. =R7 =SP =RP =IP =W" ;

\ **************************************************************************
\ Run, step, and the commands
\ **************************************************************************

: SINGLE_STEP   ( -- )          \ do one op
        AM-STEP ;

: GO-TILL       ( a -- )        \ run until AMPC = a, or the word ends
        BEGIN   AMDONE @ 0=  OVER AMPC @ <> AND
        WHILE   AM-STEP
        REPEAT  DROP ;

: TRACE_DONE    ( -- )          \ run to the end without stopping
        BEGIN   AMDONE @ 0=
        WHILE   AM-STEP
        REPEAT  ;

: SET_REGISTER  ( -- )
        CR ." { in the form $23 =R0 } command: "
        QUERY INTERPRET ;

: SET-BRK       ( -- )
        CR ." Go till address (hex): "
        HERE 1+ 12 EXPECT  SPAN @ HERE C!
        BASE @ >R HEX  HERE NUMBER?  R> BASE !
        IF      DROP GO-TILL
        ELSE    2DROP
        THEN    ;

: ABANDON       ( -- )          \ give up: ABORT (which empties the stack)
        TRUE ABORT" CODE debugger abandoned" ;

: DO_DBKEY      ( c1 -- f1 )    \ true when the word has finished
                CASE
( terminate )   $1B OF  trace_done              endof
( enter )       $0D OF  single_step             endof
( space )       $20 OF  single_step             endof
( help )        $BB OF  show_help               endof
                upc                 \ remaining tests are case insensitive
( Go )          'G' OF  set-brk                 endof
( register set) 'R' OF  set_register            endof
( debug done )  'D' OF  trace_done              endof
( abandon )     'A' OF  abandon                 endof
( help )        '?' OF  show_help               endof
( all others )          beep
                ENDCASE AMDONE @ ;

: STEPS         ( -- )          \ the main trace loop
        CR ." CODE debugger: SPACE steps, ESC runs to the end, F1 or ? help"
        AMDONE OFF
        BEGIN   show_debug
                key do_dbkey
        UNTIL
        CR ." Done:" .REGS DEBUG.S ;

: SIM>          ( -- x[n-1] .. x0  r0 .. r[m-1] m )
\ The private data stack back again, then what the word left on the
\ private return stack (deepest on top), and how many.
        DEBUG_DEPTH DUP 0<
        IF      DROP CR ." The word took more than was on the stack!"
        ELSE    DUP #SIMDS >
                IF      DROP CR ." The word left too much on the stack!"
                ELSE    0 ?DO SIMDS-TOP I 1+ CELLS - @ LOOP
                THEN
        THEN
        BP-DEPTH-DEBUG DUP 0<
        IF      DROP 0 CR ." The word popped the caller's return stack:"
                ." that can't be followed!"
        THEN
        DUP 0 ?DO 9 REG@ I CELLS + @ SWAP LOOP ;

: (AMDEBUG)     ( x... ip cfa ops -- x... r... m ip' ) \ step a CODE word
        DUP AM-RANGE FIND-LBLS          \ local labels for the display
        AMPC !  11 REG! ( W )  10 REG! ( IP )
        8 0 DO 0 I REG! LOOP
        UP @ 12 CELLS AMREGS + !
        SIMRS-TOP 9 REG!  GUARDS-OFF
        DEPTH >SIM
        STEPS
        SIM>  10 REG@ ;

\ ***************************************************************************
\ Break point control words, allow setting, removing, and displaying
\ the current break point.
\ ***************************************************************************

FORTH DEFINITIONS

VARIABLE BREAK-CFA      \ the word with a break point, or 0
VARIABLE BREAK-CT       \ and its own code token

: UNBREAK       ( -- )                  \ remove the break point
        BREAK-CFA @ ?DUP
        IF      BREAK-CT @ SWAP !CT
                BREAK-CFA OFF
        THEN    ;

: UNBREAK_BYE   ( -- )
        UNBREAK DEFERS BYEFUNC ;

' UNBREAK_BYE IS BYEFUNC        \ make break point removal automatic

: BREAK-HIT     ( pfa ip -- r... m ip' )  \ the break point word ran
        SWAP BODY> UNBREAK
        DUP >AMCODE ( ip cfa ops ) (AMDEBUG) ;

: (BREAKWORD)   ( -- )                  \ a code token for break points:
        CREATE DOES>  R@ BREAK-HIT      \ step the word, then return to
                R> DROP  SWAP           \ the IP it leaves, with what it
                BEGIN   ?DUP            \ left on the return stack
                WHILE   ROT >R 1-       \ under that
                REPEAT  >R ;

(BREAKWORD) BREAKPOINT-DUMMY
' BREAKPOINT-DUMMY @ CONSTANT BREAK-TOKEN

: ABREAKAT      ( cfa -- )              \ install a break point in a word
        UNBREAK                         \ restore previous if needed
        DUP >AMCODE 0= ABORT" Not a CODE word"
        DUP BREAK-CFA !  DUP @ BREAK-CT !
        BREAK-TOKEN SWAP !CT
        CR ." Break point set" ;

: BREAKAT       ( | <name> -- )         \ install a break point
        ' ABREAKAT ;

' BREAKAT ALIAS XX              \ xx is an alias for breakat

: .BREAK        ( -- )                  \ display the current break point
        BREAK-CFA @ ?DUP CR
        IF      ." Break point set in " >NAME .ID
        ELSE    ." No break point set"
        THEN    ;

\ **************************************************************************
\ setup for tracing a series of instructions, and call STEPS.
\ **************************************************************************

: TRACE         ( -<name>- )   \ a break point, then run the word
        ' DUP ABREAKAT EXECUTE ;

ONLY FORTH ALSO DEFINITIONS

cr cr
.( Invoke debugger directly by typing : ) cr cr
.(       TRACE <forth_name> <enter> ) cr cr
.(   or indirectly by typing: ) cr cr
.(       BREAKAT <forth_name> <enter> ) cr cr

\s
\ ***************************************************************************
\ some test words for the debugger
\ ***************************************************************************

code TST        ( -- )
        R0 23 LI,
        R0 PUSH,
        NEXT,
        END-CODE

cr .( try:  TRACE TST <enter>  )

code ++ R0 POP, R1 POP, R0 R0 R1 ADD, R0 PUSH, NEXT, END-CODE

: TEST 2 3 ++ . ;

.( try:  BREAKAT ++ <enter>  )
.( then:  TEST <enter> )
cr
